## Setup
```{r}
#| label: setup
#| message: false
library(tidyverse)
library(ggbeeswarm)
```

`library(ggbeeswarm)` is the new R package, introducing itself as the smarter version of jitter which removes randomness  

## 3.1 Data Preparation

```{r}
#| label: load-penguins-dataset
penguins <- datasets::penguins
```

`penguins <- datasets::penguins` loads the built-in R penguins dataset and not the palmerpenguins::penguins package

```{r}
#| label: penguins-dataset-dimension
nrow(penguins)
ncol(penguins)
```

`nrow(penguins)` counts the number of rows and `ncol(penguins)` counts the number of columns

a. Size of the Dataset: The size of the dataset is 344 rows and 8 columns

```{r}
#| label: data-preparation-exploration
head(penguins)
tail(penguins)
glimpse(penguins)
```

`head(penguins)` shows the first six rows of penguins dataset, `tail(penguins)` shows the last six rows of penguins dataset and `glimpse(penguins)` gives a complete summary of the dataset and allowed us to spot NAs.

b. Preliminary Exploration: Upon exploring the penguins dataset using `head()`, `tail()`, and `glimpse()`, we observed that the data is organized by species and consists of a mix of numerical measurements such as bill length and body mass, and categorical variables including species, island, and sex. 

    A key finding from the `glimpse()` and `head()` output is the presence of missing values (NA), which indicates that specific data cleaning steps will be necessary before proceeding with further analysis. Additionally, the structure reveals that the data is sorted by species, an important feature to note as it influences how observations are aggregated and visualized across the dataset.

```{r}
#| label: data-preparation-cleaning
penguins_bill <- penguins |>
  select(species, bill_len = bill_len, bill_dep = bill_dep) |>
  drop_na()

nrow(penguins_bill)
```

The pipeline above takes the penguins dataset and selects only three relevant columns using `select()`, it then removes rows with missing values using `drop_na()`

`nrow(penguins_bill)` counts the number of rows in `penguins_bill`

c. Data Cleaning: After removing all the missing values using `drop_na()`, it removed 2 rows and now we are left with 342 rows

#### Verifier Checks — 3.1

- 344 rows, 8 columns confirmed via dim(penguins)
- NAs visible in row 4 of head() 
- Factor columns: species, island, sex; numeric: rest
- anyNA(penguins_bill) returns FALSE ; no NAs remain after drop_na()
- 342 rows remain after drop_na()
- 2 rows removed confirmed via filter(`if_any(everything(), is.na)`)
- 344 - 2 == 342 returns TRUE


## 3.2 Distribution of Bill Length

### 3.2a Ungrouped Bill-Length Histogram
```{r}
#| label: bill-length-histogram
caption_source <- "Data: Gorman et al. (2014) | Palmer Station LTER (2020)"

gg_hist_bins <- function(w) {
  ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len)) +
    geom_histogram(
      binwidth = w,
      fill = "lightyellow",
      color = "darkblue"
    ) +
    geom_rug(
      aes(y = 0),
      position = position_jitter(height = 0),
      alpha = 0.5,
      sides = "b"
    ) +
    labs(
      x = "Bill Length (mm)",
      y = "Count",
      title = "Bill Length Histogram",
      caption = caption_source
    )
}

gg_hist_bins(1)
```

`caption_source` stores data source credit string to be reused

`gg_hist_bins <- function() {ggplot() + geom_histogram() + geom_rug() + labs()}` defines a reusable function taking bin width as input without rewriting the whole plot. 

Starts a ggplot mapping bill_len to the x-axis and .data$ is a safe and unambiguous way to reference a column within a function

Draws histogram bars using the bin width passed into a function

Adds a rug plot showing individual data points and adds slight horizontal jitter so overlapping ticks are visible

#### Verifier Checks — 3.2a

- Bill length range confirmed: min = 32.1mm, max = 59.6mm
- Histogram x-axis spans approximately 30–65mm — full range is displayed without clipping

### 3.2b Bin Width

```{r}
#| label: histogram-bin-width
gg_hist_bins(0.8)

gg_hist_bins(1.5)
```

Comparing bin widths of 0.8 and 1.5, we observe that the larger bin width aggregates more data points into each bar, resulting in higher counts and a smoother, more generalized appearance. 

Conversely, a smaller bin width reveals more granular detail, though it can introduce visual 'noise.' The choice of bin width significantly affects the apparent shape: too large, and important structural features may be obscured. Too small, and the distribution may appear fragmented. 

For the final report, we would select the bin width of `0.8`. This choice is preferable because it provides a more granular view that highlights the underlying distribution of bill lengths, allowing for a more accurate representation of the data's variation without sacrificing the clarity needed to identify patterns.

### 3.2c Histogram Interpretation

The histogram of bill lengths exhibits a clear multimodal structure, displaying three different peaks when using a bin width of `0.8`. This indicates that the distribution is not unimodal, as a single, uniform population would typically produce one centered peak. 

Instead, the observed structure is driven by the fact that the dataset aggregates three different penguin species, each with their own average bill lengths.

### 3.2d Raw Point Plot by Species

```{r}
#| label: raw-point-plot
labs_len_by_species <- labs(
  x = "Bill Length (mm)",
  y = "Species",
  caption = caption_source
)

ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$species)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Raw Point Plot")
```

`labs_len_by_species <- labs()` stores the shared axis labels for all species against bill kength plots to be reused

`ggplot() + geom_point() + labs_len_by_species + labs()` plots each penguin as a dot, uses default settings - no jitter

By plotting species on the y-axis and bill length on the x-axis, we observe significant overplotting. Because each species is constrained to a single discrete row, points with similar bill lengths are drawn directly on top of one another, masking the density of the observations.

However, we are able to observe that the Adelie penguins have the shortest bills and that Gentoo penguins have a wider range of lengths, including the longest bills and Chinstrap penguins seems to have the fewest data point among the 3 species.

### 3.2e Jitter Plot

```{r}
#| label: jitter-point-plot
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$species)) +
  geom_jitter(
    alpha = 0.5,
    width = 0,
    height = 0.25
  ) +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Jitter Point Plot")
```

`geom_jitter()` randomly nudges points so overlaps become visible

`alpha = 0.5` gives the transparency so dense areas seem darker

`width = 0` means no horizontal jitter

`height = 0.25` nudges points slightly up/down so they spread without crossing into another species

Using `geom_jitter()` reveals that the points were previously stacked, masking the true count of observations at each bill length. By spreading them vertically, we can now observe the 'density centers' for each species. 

We can observe that Adelie and Gentoo penguins have tightly clustered bill length distributions, while Chinstrap penguins show a more spread-out distribution with a higher concentration of individuals at longer bill lengths. The jitter allows us to visualize the local frequency of observations, highlighting that certain bill lengths are much more common within each species than others which we could not see previously.

### 3.2f Beeswarm Plot

```{r}
#| label: beeswarm-plot
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$species)) +
  geom_beeswarm(
    cex = 1.5
  ) +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Beeswarm Plot")
```

`geom_beeswarm()` offsets points and no randomness. The `cex = 1.5` controls the spacing between points 

Unlike the `geom_jitter()` plot, which relies on pseudo-random displacement to spread out overlapping points, the beeswarm plot uses a deterministic algorithm to arrange points. It stacks observations in a non-overlapping, 'swarm' pattern based on their density. 

This creates a much cleaner, more organized representation of the data distribution, as it allows us to see the exact density of bill lengths without the artificial 'noise' introduced by random jittering.

### 3.2g Pros and Cons

One advantage of the beeswarm plot is that it creates a clean, deterministic representation of bill length density, making it immediately visible that Adelie and Gentoo penguins form tightly clustered groups compared to the more dispersed Chinstrap population. This provides a much more organized and readable alternative to the random scattering of a jitter plot, where overlapping points can obscure the true density.

A disadvantage, however, is that the beeswarm's deterministic layout can lead to overcrowding. While it works well for these 342 observations, the swarm may become visually cluttered or unreadable if applied to larger datasets, where the algorithm may struggle to accommodate every point clearly.

### 3.2h Faceted Bill-Length Histogram

```{r}
#| label: faceted-histogram
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len)) +
  geom_histogram(
    bins = 20,
    fill = "lightyellow",
    color = "darkblue"
  ) +
  geom_rug(
    aes(y = 0),
    position = position_jitter(height = 0),
    alpha = 0.5,
    sides = "b"
  ) +
  facet_wrap(vars(species), ncol = 1) +
  geom_vline(aes(xintercept = mean(bill_len)), color = "red") +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Faceted Bill Length Histogram")
```

`facet_wrap()` splits the histogram into 3 stacked panels, one per species, for a distinct side by side comparison

`geom_vline()` draws a vertical red line at the overall mean bill length as the x-position reference anchor appears in all 3 facets

### 3.2i Violin-with-Box Plot
```{r}
#| label: violin-with-box-plot
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(bill_len, species)) +
  geom_violin() +
  geom_boxplot(aes(fill = species), width = 0.2) +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Violin and Box Plot")
```

`geom_violin()` draws the distribution shape

`geom_boxplot()` overlays a narrow box plot showing median, IQR, and outliers

`fill = species` colors each box by species for ease of identification

Violin plot shows the overall shape and density of the distribution

### 3.2j Why Combine Violin and Box Plot

A box plot is able to highlight summary statistics, such as the median, interquartile range (IQR), and outliers, but it fails to represent the underlying distributional shape. 

Meanwhile, a violin plot provides an excellent visualization of data density and multimodality, but it lacks the specific statistical markers necessary for precise interpretation. 

By combining both, we gain a comprehensive view that overlays clear summary statistics onto the distribution's shape, allowing us to see density patterns alongside other statistics, such as identifying the Gentoo species' outliers in a single, unified visualization.

## 3.3 Distribution of Bill Depth

### 3.3a Bill-Depth Histogram

```{r}
#| label: bill-depth-histogram
gg_depth_bins <- function(w) {
  ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_dep)) +
    geom_histogram(
      binwidth = w,
      fill = "lightyellow",
      color = "darkblue"
    ) +
    geom_rug(
      aes(y = 0),
      position = position_jitter(height = 0),
      alpha = 0.5,
      sides = "b"
    ) +
    labs(
      x = "Bill Depth (mm)",
      y = "Count",
      title = str_c("Bill Depth Histogram"),
      caption = caption_source
    )
}

gg_depth_bins(0.8)
```

When comparing the distributions, bill depth exhibits a more compact spread, ranging approximately from 13 mm to 22 mm, whereas bill length spans a much wider range of approximately 32 mm to 60 mm. 

Additionally, while the bill length distribution is distinctly multimodal, the bill depth distribution appears more unimodal or subtly skewed, indicating that the species share more overlap in their bill depth measurements than in their bill lengths.

### 3.3b Faceted Bill-Depth Histogram
```{r}
#| label: faceted-bill-depth-histogram
global_mean_dep <- mean(penguins_bill$bill_dep)

ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_dep)) +
  geom_histogram(
    binwidth = 0.8,
    fill = "lightyellow",
    color = "darkblue"
  ) +
  facet_wrap(vars(species), ncol = 1) +
  geom_rug(
    aes(y = 0),
    position = position_jitter(height = 0),
    alpha = 0.5,
    sides = "b"
  ) +
  geom_vline(aes(xintercept = global_mean_dep), color = "red") +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(
    x = "Bill Depth (mm)",
    title = "Faceted Bill Depth Histogram"
  )
```

`global_mean_dep <- mean()` calculates the overall mean bill depth across all species. As it is needed inside `geom_vline()`, the mean has to be global value pre-computed

Based of the faceted histogram, Gentoo penguins have a noticeably lower bill depth sitting well below the global mean anchor line. Adelie and Chinstrap penguins both have similar bill depths placing them close to or slightly above the global mean line. In conclusion, Gentoo penguins are clearly separable by bill depth while Adelie and Chinstrap distributions overlap noticeably

### 3.3c Comparison of Bill Length and Bill Depth

Using the Bill Length, we are able to differentiate the Adelie penguins from the Gentoo and Chinstrap penguins as the Adelie penguins have a shorter bill length compared to the other two penguin species. However, we are unable to differentiate the Chinstrap and Gentoo penguins based on Bill Length alone.

Using Bill Depth, we are able to differentiate the Gentoo penguins as they have a shorter bill depth compared to the Adelie and Chinstrap species.

Therefore the two measurements help to separate different species. 

```{r}
#| label: verifier-3.3c


```

#### Verifier Checks - 3.3c

- Species mean bill lengths: Adelie = 38.79mm, Chinstrap = 48.83mm, Gentoo = 47.50mm
- Species mean bill depths: Adelie = 18.35mm, Chinstrap = 18.42mm, Gentoo = 14.98mm
- Coder's claim holds — Adelie is separable by bill length (notably shorter mean)
- Coder's claim holds — Gentoo is separable by bill depth (notably shallower mean)
- The two measurements separate different species, consistent with the visual claims

## 3.4 Bivariate View: Bill Depth vs Bill length

### 3.4a Scatter plot
```{r}
#| label: scatter-plot-with-linear-model
labs_len_by_bills <- labs(
  x = "Bill Length (mm)",
  y = "Bill Depth (mm)",
  caption = caption_source
)

lm_plot <-
  ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$bill_dep)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(
    method = "lm"
  ) +
  labs_len_by_bills

lm_plot +
  labs(title = "Scatter plot with linear model")
```

`geom_smooth()` using the method `lm` fits and draws a linear regression line

### 3.4b Interpretation of Pooled trend

When data for all species are pooled, the regression line reveals a negative relationship between bill length and bill depth, indicating that penguins with longer bills tend to have shallower bills. 

The narrow confidence band, which maintains a distinct negative slope across the entire range of the data, suggests that this inverse relationship is statistically significant and robust

### 3.4c Exact Overplotting

```{r}
#| label: overplotting
coord_counts <- penguins_bill |>
  count(bill_len, bill_dep)

hidden_points <- sum(coord_counts$n) - nrow(coord_counts)
max_stack <- max(coord_counts$n)

hidden_points
max_stack
```

`count()` counts how many rows share each exact pair

`hidden_points <- sum() - nrow()` calculates the total rows minus number of unique positions to find number of hidden points

Exactly coincident coordinates are **not** the only cause of overplotting. While perfectly identical coordinates cause points to sit directly on top of one another, there are other causes such as:

- Data Rounding: Even if data points are not strictly identical, they may be rounded to the same precision (e.g., both measuring 45.1 mm)

- Large Dataset Size: In very large datasets, even if data points have distinct values, the sheer number of points relative to the resolution of the screen (the "drawing space") ensures that points will inevitably overlap

### 3.4d Mitigation of Overplotting

```{r}
#| label: jitter-overplot
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$bill_dep)) +
  geom_jitter(
    alpha = 0.5,
    width = 0,
    height = 0.1
  ) +
  geom_smooth(
    method = "lm"
  ) +
  labs_len_by_bills +
  labs(title = "Jittered Scatter plot")
```

`width = 0` keeps x accurate with minimal jitter to reveal density

`geom_count()` draws circles sized by how many points that share exact location

`scale_size_area()` makes circle area proportional to count which makes it mote accurate

```{r}
#| label: count-overplot
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$bill_dep)) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm") +
  geom_count(alpha = 0.5) +
  scale_size_area() +
  labs_len_by_bills +
  labs(title = "Count-symbol Plot")
```

### 3.4e Assessment of Scatter Plot Variants

For a journal submission, we would submit (d.ii) the count-symbol plot. Jittering uses a random variable into the data positions to prevent overlap, this causes a different plot to generate each time it runs

`geom_count()` provides an accurate and reproducible visual representation of the data density. It allows the user to clearly see both the underlying trend and the exact frequency of overlapping data coordinates

### 3.4f Grouped Scatter Plot

```{r}
#| label: color-scatter-plot
ggplot(
  penguins_bill,
  aes(x = .data$bill_len, y = .data$bill_dep, color = .data$species)
) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm") +
  geom_count(alpha = 0.5) +
  scale_size_area() +
  labs_len_by_bills +
  labs(
    title = "Colored Count-symbol Plot",
    color = "Species"
  )
```

Including `color = species` within the `aes()` mapping causes both the `geom_count()` and `geom_smooth()` to automatically group the data by species. Resulting in separate colored dots and separate regression lines per species 

After mapping the `species` to colour, it reveals that the negative trend observed previously was misleading.

It originally showed that there was a negative correlation, where as bill length increases, the bill depth decreases. However, for each individual species, there is actually a positive correlation between the bill length and the bill depth

The statistical phenomenon observed is called **Simpson's Paradox**.

This occurs when a trend appears in different groups of data but disappears or reverses when these groups are combined

#### Verifier Checks — 3.4c & 3.4f

- Hidden points confirmed via `distinct()`: 342 total - 338 distinct = 4 hidden points
- Matches coder's `count()` result of 4 hidden points
- Pooled correlation = -0.235 (negative) — matches the negative slope in 3.4a
- Per-species correlations all positive: Adelie = 0.391, Gentoo = 0.643, Chinstrap = 0.654


## 3.5 Summarizing the Insights gained

Yes, bill length and bill depth are highly effective at distinguishing the three Pygoscelis species. As shown in the colored count-symbol plot ([3.4f](#f-grouped-scatter-plot)), the species cluster into distinct regions, Gentoo penguins occupy a unique space characterized by lower bill depths, while Adelie and Chinstrap penguins show clear separation based on bill length, with Chinstrap penguins tending toward longer bills and Adelie penguins exhibiting shorter bills

Ignoring species leads to a misleading view because it obscures the true relationship between these two traits. When the data was combined, as seen in [3.4a](#a-scatter-plot), the regression line suggests a negative correlation, implying that as bill length increases, bill depth decreases. However, the colored plot ([3.4f](#f-grouped-scatter-plot)) reveals that within each species, the relationship is actually positive

This illustrates Simpson's Paradox, a statistical phenomenon where a trend observed within individual groups reverses when the data is aggregated. By ignoring the species as a grouping variable, the pooled analysis creates a trend that reflects the differences in mean bill dimensions between the species rather than the actual relationship between the measurements within those populations

#### Verifier Checks — 3.5 (Cross-referenced with Horst, Hill and Gorman, 2022)

- Team claims Gentoo has lower bill depth which is consistent with paper's Figure 1 showing Gentoo clustering at lower bill depth values compared to Adelie and Chinstrap
- Team claims Adelie has shorter bill length and Chinstrap tends toward longer bills which is consistent with paper's Figure 1 showing clear separation between Adelie and the other two species on bill length
- Team states Chinstrap and Gentoo cannot be separated by bill length alone. This is supported by the paper, but the team does not explicitly note that bill depth does help separate Chinstrap from Gentoo (Chinstrap has deeper bills)
- Team correctly identifies that pooled trend shows a negative correlation. The paper confirms this reversal occurs when species grouping is ignored
- Team correctly identifies within-species trends as positive. The paper's Figure 1 confirms all three species show significant positive bill length vs bill depth correlations
- Simpson's Paradox correctly named and explained. The paper highlights this as a key property of the dataset and uses it as a teaching example

## AI Liaison Report

**Tool used:** Perplexity (Claude Sonnet 4.6)

### One Useful AI Response

**In the response :**

Note: ggbeeswarm is an external package and is not part of the restricted tidyverse list. However, since the question explicitly asks you to use it, it is permitted as an exception for this specific task.


install.packages("ggbeeswarm")

library(ggbeeswarm)
library(ggplot2)

The Code
```r
#| label: bar-chart
#| echo: true
#| eval: false
ggplot(penguins_bill, aes(x = bill_len, y = species)) +
  geom_beeswarm(
    method = "swarm",
    cex = 1.5,
    alpha = 0.5,
    side = 0L,
    priority = "ascending",
    corral = "wrap"
  ) +
  labs_len_by_species +
  labs(title = "Beeswarm Plot")
```

AI is able to explain to me what beeswarm is able to do and also reminds me that it was not initally mentioned as an excepted package. Furthermore, it is able to show me how to use `geom_beeswarm()` as I had requested to help me answer the question.

### Suboptimal AI Response

**1: Prompt that seem to be incomplete/outdated:**

From the Solution:

```r
#| label: data-clean
#| echo: true
#| eval: false
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)


penguins_bill <- penguins %>%
  select(species, bill_len = bill_length_mm, bill_dep = bill_depth_mm) %>%
  drop_na()
```
Step-by-Step Breakdown

`select()` — picks only the three required columns (species, bill_length_mm, bill_depth_mm) and simultaneously renames the bill columns to bill_len and bill_dep using the new_name = old_name syntax

`drop_na()` — called with no arguments after select(), so it checks all columns in the already-narrowed tibble and drops any row where any of the three columns has an NA

The result is automatically a tibble since penguins is already a tibble and dplyr verbs preserve that class.


**2: Stubbornness in AI:**

In the section of "Here is the updated solution using tibble() explicitly:"

Solution
```r
#| label: data-clean
#| echo: true
#| eval: false
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(tibble)
library(palmerpenguins)

penguins_bill <- penguins %>%
  select(species, bill_len = bill_length_mm, bill_dep = bill_depth_mm) %>%
  drop_na() %>%
  as_tibble()
```
 
AI kept suggesting that penguins_bill was already a tribble therefore did not initially use the tibble() as per requested. After being prompted again, it continued to suggest as_tibble() first before helping me with a solution to use a proper tibble().

### Reflection

Overall the AI was very useful in explaining unfamiliar functions and also helping the team understand how to use certain functions better to complete the task at hand. Furthermore, the team utilised it alot in the explanation/reporting and theory question which increased our understanding of the topic, the limitation, formatting and answering our simple and/or complicated "why" questions. 

The AI did assume the type of penguin package to be the one from 'palmerpenguins' which resulted in confusion for us.  (Causing the team to use the wrong column names at the start of the lab).

As a result, using AI was very helpful during this lab session however, one needs to be aware of the objectives of the work they do and learn to verify answers using diagnostic commands. 

